-- title:
--   Existence of lawful split coordinates in normal form
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a commutative ring, $p$ a prime such that $(p:\mathcal{O})$ is a non-zero-divisor, equipped with an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra structure on $\mathbb{Z}/p$ whose structure map has kernel the ideal $(p)$, and assume $\mathcal{O}$ is adically complete for $(p)$. Fix $r$ and a Honda system $H_1$ for the element $(p:\mathcal{O})$ on $\mathcal{O}^r = (\mathrm{Fin}\,r \to \mathcal{O})$, that is, $\mathcal{O}$-linear $F,V$ with $FV = VF = p\cdot\mathrm{id}$ together with a submodule $L$ satisfying: every element of $L$ lying in the range of $F$ is $p$ times an element of $L$; $p\,y \in \operatorname{range} F$ for $y \in L$; $\operatorname{range} F + L = \top$; and $V$ is injective on $L$. Let $G$ be a tower of commutative rings that are finite cocommutative Hopf algebras over $\mathbb{Z}/p$, with bialgebra transition maps $s_v : G(v+1) \to G(v)$ that are surjective, with $\dim_{\mathbb{Z}/p} G(v) = p^{vr}$ and $\ker s_v$ the $p^v$-torsion ideal of $G(v+1)$ (the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^v$), and with each Cartier dual, i.e. the $\mathbb{Z}/p$-dual of $G(v)$, a local ring. Let $\pi_v : \mathcal{O}^r \to \mathrm{DieudonneModule}(\mathbb{Z}/p, p, G(v))$ be additive, surjective, with $\pi_v x = 0$ exactly when $x \in p^v\mathcal{O}^r$, carrying $F$ and $V$ to Frobenius and Verschiebung on the Dieudonné module, and compatible with the transitions in the sense that the map induced by $s_v$ sends $\pi_{v+1}x$ to $\pi_v x$. Then there exists a system of split coordinates $\mathcal{S}$ for $(p,r,H_1,G,s,\pi)$ which is both `Lawful` and in `NormalForm`. The data $\mathcal{S}$ consists of integers $d$, $h^c$, $h^e$; towers $G^c$, $G^e$ of Hopf algebras over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ with transitions $s^c$, $s^e$; maps $q^c_v : G(v) \to G^c(v)$, $\pi^e_v : G(v) \to G^e(v)$, a section $\sigma_v : G^e(v) \to G(v)$ and $\Theta_v : G(v) \to G^c(v) \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}/p} G^e(v)$; a $d$-variable formal group law $\Phi_0$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ with presentations $\kappa_v$ of $G^c(v)$ by $\mathbb{Z}/p$-power series in $d$ variables; a tower $E^t$ of Hopf algebras over $\mathcal{O}$ with transitions $s^t$ and comparison maps $\theta^e_v : \mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{\mathcal{O}} E^t(v) \to G^e(v)$; covector data $\hat c$; submodules $M^c$, $M^{et}$ of $\mathcal{O}^r$; an $\mathcal{O}$-basis $\alpha$ of $H_1.L$ indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,d$; and power-series families $\bar a$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ and $a$ over $\mathcal{O}$. Lawfulness is a long list of conditions on these data, summarised here: $h^c + h^e = r$; each $G^c(v)$ is local and each $G^e(v)$ is reduced, both towers being finite, cocommutative, with surjective transitions, ranks $p^{vh^c}$ and $p^{vh^e}$ and kernels the respective $p^v$-torsion ideals; $q^c_v$ and $\pi^e_v$ are surjective with $\ker \pi^e_v$ the nilradical of $G(v)$, $\sigma_v$ followed by $\pi^e_v$ the identity, $\ker q^c_v$ the ideal generated by the image under $\sigma_v$ of the kernel of the counit of $G^e(v)$, and $\Theta_v$ the comultiplication followed by $q^c_v \otimes \pi^e_v$, bijective; compatibility of $q^c$, $\pi^e$, $\sigma$, $\Theta$ with the transitions; formal unramifiedness of $G^e(v)$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p$; commutativity of $\Phi_0$, surjectivity of $\kappa_v$ with kernel generated by the $p^v$-series of $\Phi_0$, compatibility of $\kappa$ with $s^c$, vanishing of the counit on $\kappa_v(X_i)$, $\kappa_v(X_i)$ in the radical of $(p)$, $\kappa_v$ computed by adic evaluation, and the corresponding conditions on the comultiplication; and further conditions relating $E^t$, $\theta^e$, $\hat c$, $M^c$, $M^{et}$, $\alpha$, $\bar a$ and $a$. Being in normal form asserts two conditions on the lifted series $a$: the linear part of $(a_i 0)_i$ reduces to the identity matrix modulo $(p)$, and the $(i,j)$ entry of the linear part of $(a_i 1)_i$ lies in $(p)$ whenever $j \le i$.
--
--   This is the data-gathering step of Fontaine's proof of his lifting theorem for unipotent $p$-divisible groups over $\mathbb{F}_p$: it assembles, in one structure, the connected–étale splitting of the tower, coordinates on the connected part in the style of Tate, an étale lift over $\mathcal{O}$, the Fitting-type splitting of the Honda module and a normalising change of coordinates. It is used by [`Deformation.HondaSystem.exists_pDivisibleTower_bijective_map_mem_fontaineHodge_of_pDivisibleTower_zmod`](thm.html#Deformation.HondaSystem.exists_pDivisibleTower_bijective_map_mem_fontaineHodge_of_pDivisibleTower_zmod), the formal counterpart of the lifting theorem itself.
